<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Teams on Layer5 Documentation</title><link>/pr-preview/pr-1326/tags/teams/</link><description>Recent content in Teams on Layer5 Documentation</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="/pr-preview/pr-1326/tags/teams/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Teams</title><link>/pr-preview/pr-1326/cloud/concepts/identity-and-security/teams/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/pr-preview/pr-1326/cloud/concepts/identity-and-security/teams/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A team is a named group of users inside one organization. Teams exist so an administrator can give a set of people the same access once, instead of granting it person by person. A team carries two kinds of access: &lt;strong&gt;roles&lt;/strong&gt; held by its members, and &lt;strong&gt;workspace assignments&lt;/strong&gt; that give the whole team access to a workspace and the resources in it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Organizations contain any number of teams. A team has any number of members, and a user can belong to any number of teams. Teams are flat: a team cannot contain another team.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Manage Teams</title><link>/pr-preview/pr-1326/cloud/guides/organizations/managing-teams/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/pr-preview/pr-1326/cloud/guides/organizations/managing-teams/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;This guide covers the administrator tasks for teams. For what a team is and how it grants access, read the &lt;a href="/pr-preview/pr-1326/cloud/concepts/identity-and-security/teams/"&gt;Teams concept&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;














